## STEP 3 — HYPOTHESES

**[HYP] npm gladia@0.1.3 — orphaned impersonator with API key leakage in WebSocket URL**
- class: OTHER
- asset: npm registry — `gladia@0.1.3`
- confidence: 95
- reasoning: Package claims "Official TypeScript SDK" in package.json but README says "Unofficial". Source repo `alexisbouchez/gladia.ts` + GitHub user both 404 (orphaned, irrevocable takeover risk). `src/client.ts:306-308` appends raw `x-gladia-key` to `wss://api.gladia.io/v2/live` URL query via `searchParams.append`, diverging from official SDK's POST-then-token flow. dist-tag latest=0.1.3 persists.
- evidence_needed: Already complete — tarball sha256 `3b23ec7d7a763abc04c52db232d157a982fd3bd969c9f703af3eecad5fa802f2`, shasum `cc96f84a200c0fd49a71e919391f9b659c39f3e9`, npm view metadata, GitHub API 404 on repo+user, RAG of client.ts:306-308.
- verify_steps: `npm view gladia@0.1.3` (metadata), `curl -s https://registry.npmjs.org/gladia/0.1.3 | jq .` (shasum/dist), `curl -s https://api.github.com/repos/alexisbouchez/gladia.ts` (404), `curl -s https://api.github.com/users/alexisbouchez` (404). All passive, all already executed across multiple cycles.
- impact: Supply-chain attack surface — attacker who claims orphaned `alexisbouchez` GitHub account or `gladia` npm namespace could push malicious update to `latest` tag. Current version leaks API keys in WebSocket URL query params (logged in server access logs, proxy logs, browser history). Severity: Medium (P3/P4).
- testability: PASSIVE

**[HYP] api.gladia.io — SSRF via audio_url/video_url with no scheme allowlist**
- class: SSRF
- asset: api.gladia.io — POST /v2/pre-recorded (audio_url, video_url, callback_url)
- confidence: 73
- reasoning: OpenAPI spec confirms audio_url/video_url/callback_url/CallbackConfig.url all `format:uri` with no scheme allowlist. SDK RAG confirms no client-side guard (is_url() only gates upload-vs-direct path). /v1/models confirms FR/US egress. 7 webhook delivery paths to client-supplied URLs. Key is sole gate.
- evidence_needed: Valid API key to test SSRF payload delivery to internal metadata endpoint (169.254.169.254) or internal network.
- verify_steps: AUTH_HELPED — requires valid `x-gladia-key`. With key: `POST /v2/pre-recorded` with `audio_url: "http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/"` and observe if server fetches it. Passive probe without key only confirms 401 gate.
- impact: Server-side request forgery — attacker with valid key could scan internal network, access cloud metadata endpoints, or reach internal services. Severity: High (P2) if exploitable.
- testability: AUTH_HELPED

**[HYP] app.gladia.io — post-auth open redirect via redirect_to**
- class: OATH
- asset: app.gladia.io — /signin?redirect_to=
- confidence: 45
- reasoning: Unauthenticated layer reflects redirect_to into form action (confirmed across 50+ cycles). CSP lacks form-action directive. OAuth redirect_uri is FIXED (PKCE S256) — no OAuth code theft. Return-to cookie tamper-reset confirmed (REJECTED). Sole remaining question: does server honor redirect_to after successful authentication?
- evidence_needed: Authenticated session to observe post-auth Location header.
- verify_steps: HUMAN_ONLY — requires valid credentials or OAuth completion to test post-auth redirect honoring. Passive probe only confirms unauthenticated reflection.
- impact: If post-auth honoring confirmed, attacker could craft login URL redirecting victim to attacker-controlled site after successful auth. Severity: Low-Medium (P3/P4).
- testability: HUMAN_ONLY

